      "content": "<img src='2604.17337v1/x4.png' alt='Refer to caption' title='' width='660' height='377' />\n\n*Figure 3: Overview of AutoSearch, which adaptively selects the search depth to trade off accuracy and efficiency.*\n\nIn this section, we present AutoSearch, a self-answer-driven RL framework for adaptive search in agentic RAG systems.\nUnlike prior approaches that separately optimize answer quality and search efficiency, AutoSearch enables the agent to jointly improve both with self-evaluative signals.\nFig.[3](#S4.F3 \"Figure 3 ‣ 4 Autosearch ‣ AutoSearch: Adaptive Search Depth for Efficient Agentic RAG via Reinforcement Learning\") provides an overview of AutoSearch, which incorporates three complementary reward signals:\n(i) a base reward that regularizes search behavior,\n(ii) a search efficiency reward that improves search efficiency while preserving answer quality, and\n(iii) a search quality reward that enhances search quality.\nThe agent is trained with these rewards to balance search depth and search capability in an adaptive manner, accounting for both question complexity and its evolving answer capability.\n\n### 4.1 Base reward\n\nWe introduce a *base reward* to ensure stable multi-step retrieval and accurate answers, comprising a *format reward* to enforce structured actions and an *outcome reward* to encourage correct answers.\nThe format reward enforces a structured action format that separates reasoning, retrieval, and final answering.\nDuring retrieval steps, an action is considered invalid if it violates the prescribed format.\nAdditionally, repeated retrieval of previously observed information is also treated as invalid, discouraging redundant search behavior.\nAt the final step, only the format of the produced answer is evaluated.\nRather than assigning a single reward to the entire trajectory, we compute the validity of each step independently to enable fine-grained credit assignment.\nFormally, for the $i$-th question at step $t$, we define a binary indicator $\\mathrm{valid}_{i,t}\\in{0,1}$, indicating whether the action at step $t$ is well-formed and non-redundant.\nThe step-wise format reward is then defined as:\n\n|  | $r_{i,t}^{\\text{format}}\\=\\begin{cases}0,\\&\\mathrm{valid}_{i,t}\\=1,\\ t<T,\\\\ -0.05,\\&\\mathrm{valid}_{i,t}\\=0,\\ t<T,\\\\ 0.1,\\&\\mathrm{valid}_{i,t}\\=1,\\ t\\=T,\\\\ -0.5,\\&\\mathrm{valid}_{i,t}\\=0,\\ t\\=T,\\end{cases}$ |  | (1) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nwhere $T$ denotes the terminal step.\nThe outcome reward measures answer correctness using the Exact Match (EM) metric between the agent’s predicted answer $a_{\\text{pred}}$ and the ground-truth answer $a_{\\text{gold}}$:\n\n|  | $r^{\\text{result}}_{i,t}\\=\\begin{cases}\\text{EM}(a_{i}^{\\text{pred}},a_{i}^{\\text{gold}}),\\&t\\=T,\\\\ 0,\\&t<T.\\end{cases}$ |  | (2) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nThe overall base reward at step $t$ is computed as the sum of the format and outcome rewards:\n\n|  | $r_{i,t}^{\\text{base}}\\=r_{i,t}^{\\text{format}}+r_{i,t}^{\\text{result}}.$ |  | (3) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n### 4.2 Search Efficiency Reward\n\nTo improve search efficiency while maintaining answer quality, we propose a search efficiency reward that adaptively determines search depth based on the agent’s capability.\nTo ensure answer quality, we generate intermediate answers at each retrieval step to identify the capability-aware optimal search depth, defined as the minimal number of retrieval steps required for the agent to answer correctly.\nBased on this depth, we design a reward function that encourages the agent to reduce its search depth while penalizing redundant retrievals, thereby improving search efficiency.\n\n#### Intermediate Answer Generation.\n\nTo estimate the optimal search depth for each trajectory based on current agent’s capability, the agent produces an intermediate answer at each retrieval step.\nFor a multi-step search process on a given question $q_{i}$, at step $t$ the agent produces an intermediate answer based on all accumulated trajectory information:\n\n|  | $x_{i,t}\\={q_{i},s_{i,0},o_{i,0},\\ldots,s_{i,t},o_{i,t}},$ |  | (4) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nwhere $s_{i,t}$ and $o_{i,t}$ denote the search query and the retrieved observation at step $t$, respectively.\nAt each retrieval step $t$, the current policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$ is prompted using Prompt 1 to produce the intermediate answer:\n\n|  | $a^{\\text{inter}}_{i,t}\\=\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{i,t}),$ |  | (5) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nwhich serves as a self-evaluation signal guiding the assessment of both search depth and quality.\n\n\n\n#### Capability-aware Optimal Search Depth.\n\nUsing the sequence of intermediate answers, we identify the capability-aware optimal search depth $t_{c}$.\nSpecifically, $t_{c}$ is defined as the earliest step at which the intermediate answer $a^{\\text{inter}}_{i,t}$ exactly matches the ground-truth answer $a_{\\text{gold}}$:\n\n|  | $t_{c}\\=\\min\\left{t\\mid\\text{EM}(a^{\\text{inter}}_{i,t},a_{\\text{gold}})\\=1\\right}.$ |  | (6) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nIf the agent fails to answer correctly, we set $t_{c}\\=-1$.\nThis depth represents the minimal retrieval steps required for the agent to answer the question correctly, reflecting its capability for the given task.\n\n*Table 1:  (Main Results) EM, F1, Search Depth and Search Efficiency Comparison Across Methods.*\n\n| Method | General QA | | | | | | Multi-Hop QA | | | | | | | |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| | NQ | | TriviaQA | | PopQA | | HotpotQA | | 2Wiki | | Bamboogle | | Avg | |\n| EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ |\n| \\rowcolorgreen!10Base model: Qwen2.5-3b-Base/Instruct | | | | | | | | | | | | | | |\n| Search-R1-base | 36.0 | 44.9 | 51.3 | 59.0 | 37.0 | 41.3 | 27.9 | 36.7 | 29.3 | 34.6 | 16.1 | 25.9 | 32.9 | 40.4 |\n| Search-R1-instruct | 32.2 | 42.0 | 52.4 | 60.3 | 35.7 | 41.2 | 30.3 | 39.6 | 32.2 | 38.0 | 25.0 | 36.9 | 34.6 | 43.0 |\n| StepSearch-base | 30.0 | 39.9 | 47.3 | 55.0 | 32.2 | 37.1 | 32.1 | 42.6 | 37.1 | 42.8 | 33.0 | 41.9 | 35.3 | 43.2 |\n| StepSearch-instruct | 33.9 | 43.5 | 51.1 | 59.3 | 34.9 | 40.2 | 33.2 | 44.2 | 34.7 | 41.5 | 33.9 | 44.7 | 37.0 | 45.6 |\n| HIPRAG-base | 11.0 | 20.2 | 23.4 | 34.1 | 14.9 | 23.0 | 10.3 | 19.1 | 8.30 | 16.2 | 10.5 | 16.8 | 13.1 | 21.6 |\n| HIPRAG-instruct | 3.20 | 9.50 | 14.5 | 24.2 | 6.60 | 13.7 | 10.1 | 18.4 | 8.70 | 18.6 | 4.00 | 11.9 | 7.85 | 16.1 |\n| \\rowcoloryellow!15 AutoSearch | 43.7 | 52.2 | 61.7 | 69.0 | 46.0 | 50.1 | 39.4 | 50.5 | 41.6 | 47.7 | 33.1 | 44.7 | 44.3 | 52.4 |\n|  | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ |\n| Search-R1-base | 1.23 | 29.4 | 1.29 | 39.9 | 1.35 | 27.4 | 1.63 | 17.2 | 1.95 | 15.0 | 1.69 | 9.50 | 1.52 | 23.1 |\n| Search-R1-instruct | 1.76 | 18.3 | 1.62 | 32.3 | 1.72 | 20.7 | 2.09 | 14.5 | 2.29 | 14.1 | 1.98 | 12.6 | 1.91 | 18.8 |\n| StepSearch-base | 2.95 | 10.2 | 2.90 | 16.3 | 2.94 | 10.9 | 3.19 | 10.1 | 3.51 | 10.6 | 3.03 | 10.9 | 3.09 | 11.5 |\n| StepSearch-instruct | 1.46 | 23.3 | 1.57 | 32.7 | 1.45 | 24.1 | 2.05 | 16.2 | 2.29 | 15.1 | 2.07 | 16.4 | 1.82 | 21.3 |\n| HIPRAG-base | 1.59 | 6.92 | 1.60 | 14.6 | 1.68 | 8.88 | 1.66 | 6.22 | 1.70 | 4.87 | 1.72 | 6.11 | 1.66 | 7.93 |\n| HIPRAG-instruct | 1.28 | 2.50 | 1.32 | 11.0 | 1.35 | 4.88 | 1.58 | 6.40 | 1.84 | 4.74 | 1.56 | 2.57 | 1.49 | 5.35 |\n| \\rowcoloryellow!15 AutoSearch | 1.03 | 42.4 | 1.05 | 58.7 | 1.07 | 42.9 | 1.49 | 26.5 | 1.69 | 24.7 | 1.83 | 18.1 | 1.36 | 35.6 |\n| \\rowcolorgreen!10Base model: Qwen2.5-7b-Base/Instruct | | | | | | | | | | | | | | |\n|  | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ | EM $\\uparrow$ | F1 $\\uparrow$ |\n| Search-R1-base | 47.6 | 56.3 | 63.7 | 71.4 | 45.7 | 49.8 | 43.7 | 55.4 | 38.7 | 44.8 | 41.1 | 53.4 | 46.8 | 55.2 |\n| Search-R1-instruct | 40.2 | 48.5 | 61.0 | 68.4 | 40.5 | 45.4 | 38.7 | 49.0 | 32.5 | 38.1 | 37.1 | 49.6 | 41.7 | 49.8 |\n| StepSearch-base | 37.7 | 47.9 | 54.7 | 63.3 | 38.6 | 44.0 | 37.0 | 48.6 | 40.9 | 47.3 | 42.7 | 51.9 | 41.9 | 50.5 |\n| StepSearch-instruct | 34.2 | 44.5 | 51.6 | 59.9 | 34.9 | 40.1 | 38.9 | 50.4 | 39.8 | 46.3 | 40.3 | 52.4 | 40.0 | 48.9 |\n| HIPRAG-instruct | 30.6 | 42.0 | 55.8 | 65.2 | 34.2 | 42.4 | 30.5 | 42.7 | 29.8 | 38.3 | 34.7 | 47.8 | 35.9 | 46.4 |\n| \\rowcoloryellow!15 AutoSearch | 47.6 | 56.0 | 65.8 | 73.1 | 46.3 | 50.3 | 42.7 | 54.2 | 43.6 | 50.3 | 41.9 | 54.9 | 48.0 | 56.5 |\n|  | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ | SD $\\downarrow$ | SE $\\uparrow$ |\n| Search-R1-base | 1.98 | 24.0 | 1.86 | 34.2 | 1.95 | 23.4 | 2.65 | 16.5 | 3.15 | 12.3 | 2.56 | 16.1 | 2.36 | 21.1 |\n| Search-R1-instruct | 1.95 | 20.7 | 1.74 | 35.0 | 1.79 | 22.6 | 2.43 | 15.9 | 3.08 | 10.5 | 2.42 | 15.3 | 2.24 | 20.0 |\n| StepSearch-base | 1.68 | 22.4 | 1.73 | 31.6 | 1.57 | 24.7 | 2.07 | 17.8 | 2.29 | 17.9 | 2.07 | 20.7 | 1.90 | 22.5 |\n| StepSearch-instruct | 1.94 | 17.6 | 1.99 | 25.9 | 1.98 | 17.6 | 2.10 | 18.6 | 2.46 | 16.2 | 2.02 | 19.9 | 2.08 | 19.3 |\n| HIPRAG-instruct | 1.07 | 28.5 | 0.97 | 57.8 | 1.16 | 29.5 | 1.55 | 19.7 | 2.15 | 13.8 | 1.45 | 23.9 | 1.39 | 28.9 |\n| \\rowcoloryellow!15 AutoSearch | 1.05 | 45.3 | 1.04 | 63.3 | 1.09 | 40.8 | 1.34 | 31.2 | 1.58 | 25.9 | 1.53 | 27.4 | 1.27 | 39.0 |\n\n#### Search Efficiency Reward.\n\nWe categorize each retrieval step into three types: (i) Under-search: If the agent fails to answer the question correctly ($t_{c}>T$), all steps in the trajectory are considered under-search. Each step is assigned a small positive reward to encourage further information acquisition. (ii) Effective search: If the agent answers correctly at step $t_{c}$, the steps preceding $t_{c}$ are considered effective search. Each of these steps receives a positive reward, with higher rewards for shorter $t_{c}$ to encourage more efficient search. (iii) Over-searching: Steps after $t_{c}$ are considered over-searching and are penalized to discourage redundant retrieval.\nFormally, the search efficiency reward at step $t$ is defined as\n\n|  | $r^{\\text{efficient}}_{i,t}\\=\\begin{cases}0.025,\\&t_{c}<0,\\\\ \\frac{0.4}{t_{c}+\\epsilon}-0.05,\\&0<t\\leq t_{c}<T-1,\\\\ -0.1,\\&0<t_{c}<t<T-1,\\end{cases}$ |  | (7) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nwhere $\\epsilon$ is a small constant for numerical stability.\nFig.[4](#S4.F4 \"Figure 4 ‣ Search Efficiency Reward. ‣ Capability-aware Optimal Search Depth. ‣ 4.2 Search Efficiency Reward ‣ 4 Autosearch ‣ AutoSearch: Adaptive Search Depth for Efficient Agentic RAG via Reinforcement Learning\") shows that, under a fixed agent capability, cumulative Efficiency reward is maximized when search terminates at the capability-aware optimal depth.\nBoth insufficient searching and over-searching lead to lower rewards, encouraging the agent to learn an optimal search depth rather than simply minimizing retrieval steps.\n\n<img src='2604.17337v1/x5.png' alt='Refer to caption' title='' width='317' height='250' />\n\n*Figure 4: Cumulative reward versus search depth. The cumulative reward peaks at optimal search depth and decreases for insufficient searching and over-searching.*\n\n### 4.3 Search Quality Reward\n\nTo improve search quality in complex tasks, we introduce a search quality reward based on intermediate answers.\nWe quantify search quality by computing the F1 score between the intermediate answer and the ground-truth answer.\nLet $PN$, $RN$, and $IN$ denote the number of tokens in the intermediate answer, the ground-truth answer, and their overlap, respectively.\nThe F1 score is defined as:\n\n|  | $\\mathrm{F1}(a^{\\text{inter}}_{i,t},a^{\\text{gold}}_{i})\\=\\frac{2\\cdot IN}{PN+RN}.$ |  | (8) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nWe define the search quality reward as the gain over the best F1 score achieved in previous steps:\n\n|  | $\\normalsize r^{\\text{quality}}_{i,t}\\=\\mathrm{F1}(a^{\\text{inter}}_{i,t},a^{\\text{gold}}_{i})-\\max_{t^{\\prime}<t}\\mathrm{F1}(a^{\\text{inter}}_{i,t^{\\prime}},a^{\\text{gold}}_{i}).$ |  | (9) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nThis reward explicitly measures the marginal contribution of each retrieval step to answer quality, encouraging the agent to perform searches that provide meaningful and non-redundant information.\n\n### 4.4 RL Training\n\nWe train the agent using a composite reward that jointly encourages answer correctness and search efficiency.\nSpecifically, the overall reward at retrieval step $t$ is defined as:\n\n|  | $r^{\\text{overall}}_{i,t}\\=r^{\\text{base}}_{i,t}+r^{\\text{efficient}}_{i,t}+r^{\\text{quality}}_{i,t},$ |  | (10) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nwhere the base reward ensures answer correctness, the efficiency reward encourages effective search trajectories, and the quality reward promotes informative retrieval steps.\nWe adopt Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) as our RL backbone, while masking external observations. We apply a token-level mask $I(o_{t})$ to exclude retrieved tokens from the training loss. The training objective is:\n\n|  | $\\mathcal{J}_{\\text{PPO}}(\\theta)\\=\\mathbb{E}_{\\begin{subarray}{c}(q,a)\\sim\\mathcal{D}\\\\ o\\sim\\pi_{\\text{old}}(\\cdot|q)\\end{subarray}}\\left[\\frac{1}{\\sum_{t\\=1}^{|o|}I(o_{t})}\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}t\\=1\\\\ I(o_{t})\\=1\\end{subarray}}^{|o_{i}|}L_{t}\\right],$ |  | (11) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nwhere $o$ denotes the search trajectory for question $q$, $|o|$ denotes the trajectory length, and loss $L_{t}$ is:\n\n|  | $L_{t}\\=\\min!\\left(r_{t}\\hat{A}_{t},\\,\\text{clip}(r_{t},\\,1-\\varepsilon,\\,1+\\varepsilon)\\hat{A}_{t}\\right),$ |  | (12) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\nwhere $r_{t}$ is importance sampling ratio and $\\hat{A}_{t}$ denotes the advantage estimate computed using GAE.",
